{"score": 3, "explanation": "Functionally, this code isn't badly broken: because HMAC is deterministic and collision-resistant, HMAC(secret, stored_token) will only equal HMAC(secret, provided_token) when the two tokens are actually equal, so the expiry check plus a hmac.compare_digest at the end does correctly (and in constant time) validate that the provided token matches the stored one before expiry. So there's no exploitable timing leak or authentication bypass in what's shown.

The issue is conceptual: signing both values with the *same* secret_key and then comparing the two resulting digests adds no real security over just doing `hmac.compare_digest(stored_token.encode(), provided_token.encode())` directly — the secret key cancels out of the comparison entirely, since it's applied identically to both sides. HMAC is meant to bind a value to a secret so an attacker without the secret can't forge a valid signature for arbitrary data; here, both inputs are already known to the function and hashed with the same key, so the 'signature' step is essentially a no-op wrapper around a direct string comparison. This is a fairly common cargo-cult pattern — using a cryptographic primitive because it 'looks secure' without it actually contributing anything beyond what a plain constant-time compare already gives you.

That points more toward a conceptual/ability gap (not fully understanding what HMAC buys you here and why the two computations are redundant) than a simple attention lapse, since the mistake is baked into the whole design of the function rather than being an isolated slip like a wrong operator or missed edge case. However, it's a fairly forgivable and non-catastrophic misunderstanding — the code still 'works' as an equality check — so I wouldn't call it a severe knowledge gap either. That's why I land closer to the ability-gap end but not at the extreme."}

